2. Global "Drag End" Listener (Line 115)
This useEffect detects when the user releases the mouse anywhere in the window. It commits the temporary liveOrder to the actual application state via onCommitCategoryOrder or onCommitProductOrder.


code
TypeScript
useEffect(() => {
    const handleGlobalDragEnd = () => {
        if (draggedItem) {
            if (draggedItem.type === 'category' && liveCategoryOrderRef.current && onCommitCategoryOrder) {
                // Commit Category Reorder
                onCommitCategoryOrder(liveCategoryOrderRef.current);
                wasDroppedRef.current = true;
            } 
            else if (draggedItem.type === 'product' && draggedItem.group && liveProductOrderRef.current && liveProductOrderRef.current[draggedItem.group] && onCommitProductOrder) {
                // Commit Product Reorder
                onCommitProductOrder(draggedItem.group, liveProductOrderRef.current[draggedItem.group]);
                wasDroppedRef.current = true;
            }
            clearDragState();
        }
    };

    if (draggedItem) {
        window.addEventListener('dragend', handleGlobalDragEnd);
        window.addEventListener('drop', handleGlobalDragEnd); 
        window.addEventListener('mouseup', handleGlobalDragEnd);
    }
    // ... cleanup ...
}, [draggedItem, onCommitCategoryOrder, onCommitProductOrder, /*...*/]);



3. Event Handlers (Line 425)
These functions handle the actual interaction. handleDragOver is the most important as it calculates where the item should move visually while dragging.


code
